<problem>

<setup>
<selectFromSequence name="a1mag" from="2" to="7" numToSelect="2" />

<select name="a1sign">
  <option>
    <number>-1</number>
    <c>
    <math simplify="numbers" name="m1">
      ($b1[2]$x[2]+$b1[4])
      ^$a1sign[1][5]
      /($b1[1]$x[1]+$b1[3])
      ^$a1sign[1][6]
    </math>
    <text extend="$m1.text" />
    </c>
    <math simplify="numbers">$b1[2]*$x[2]+$b1[4]</math>
    <math simplify="numbers">$b1[1]*$x[1]+$b1[3]</math>
    <math>$a2</math>
    <math>-$a1</math>
  </option>
  <option>
    <number>1</number>
    <c>
    <math simplify="numbers" name="m2">
      ($b1[1]$x[1]+$b1[3])
      ^$a1sign[1][5]
      /($b1[2]$x[2]+$b1[4])
      ^$a1sign[1][6]
    </math>
    <text extend="$m2.text" />
    </c>
    <math simplify="numbers">$b1[1]*$x[1]+$b1[3]</math>
    <math simplify="numbers">$b1[2]*$x[2]+$b1[4]</math>
    <math>$a1</math>
    <math>-$a2</math>
  </option>
</select>
<math name="a1" simplify="numbers">$a1mag[1]*$a1sign[1][1]</math>
<math name="a2" simplify="numbers">-$a1mag[2]*$a1sign[1][1]</math>
<selectFromSequence name="b1" exclude="0" numToSelect="4" withReplacement="true" from="-5" to="5" />

<select name="x" numToSelect="2" withReplacement="false">x y z</select>

<select name="A">A B C D</select>
<math name="log_polynomial" simplify="numbers">$a1*log($b1[1]*$x[1]+$b1[3])+$a2*log($b1[2]*$x[2]+$b1[4])</math>
<math name="single_log_arg" simplify="numbers">($b1[1]*$x[1]+$b1[3])^$a1*($b1[2]*$x[2]+$b1[4])^$a2</math>
<math name="single_log_arg2" simplify="numbers">($b1[1]*$x[1]+$b1[3])^$a1/($b1[2]*$x[2]+$b1[4])^(-$a2)</math>
<math name="single_log_arg3" simplify="numbers">($b1[2]*$x[2]+$b1[4])^$a2/($b1[1]*$x[1]+$b1[3])^(-$a1)</math>
<math name="single_log_arg4" simplify="numbers">1/(($b1[2]*$x[2]+$b1[4])^(-$a2)*($b1[1]*$x[1]+$b1[3])^(-$a1))</math>

<math name="single_log" simplify="numbers">log(($b1[1]*$x[1]+$b1[3])^$a1*($b1[2]*$x[2]+$b1[4])^$a2)</math>

</setup>
  
Rewrite the expression
<me>$log_polynomial</me>
as a single logarithm <m>\log $A</m>.  Then,
<p><m>$A =</m> <mathInput name="input1" /></p>
<p>(Note: <q>log</q> is not part of your answer.)</p>

<answer>
  <award symbolicEquality="true" simplifyOnCompare="numbers" referencesAreResponses="$input1"><when>
    $input1 = $single_log_arg
  </when></award>
  <award symbolicEquality="true" simplifyOnCompare="numbers"><when>
    $input1 = $single_log_arg2
  </when></award>
  <award symbolicEquality="true" simplifyOnCompare="numbers"><when>
    $input1 = $single_log_arg3
  </when></award>
  <award symbolicEquality="true" simplifyOnCompare="numbers"><when>
    $input1 = $single_log_arg4
  </when></award>
</answer>

<solution>
<setup>
  <math name="log_with_exponents" simplify="numbers">log(($b1[1]*$x[1]+$b1[3])^$a1)+log(($b1[2]*$x[2]+$b1[4])^$a2)</math>

</setup>
  
By the properties of logarithms, multiplication of a logarithm can be changed to the logarithm of a power.  We transform both terms:
<me>$log_polynomial \\= $log_with_exponents.</me>
Then, since the sum of logarithms is equal to the logarithm of the product, we can rewrite
<me>$log_with_exponents\\
   = $single_log.</me>
If the expression is equal to <m>\log $A</m>, then
<me>$A=$single_log_arg.</me>

</solution>

</problem>